WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.835
What the trainer actually learns from.

2
00:00:02.115 --> 00:00:10.307
Supervised fine-tuning minimizes next-token loss on your examples: the same objective as pretraining, applied to your text.

3
00:00:10.587 --> 00:00:15.771
Which tokens count toward the loss is a setting, and its default may not be what you expect.

4
00:00:16.051 --> 00:00:34.056
TRL’s documentation is explicit: for a conversational language-modeling dataset (a plain messages list), the default computes loss on the whole sequence, system prompt and user document included, unless you turn on assistant-only loss; for a prompt-completion dataset,

5
00:00:34.336 --> 00:00:37.195
the default computes loss on the completion only.

6
00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:47.651
The trainer applies the model’s chat template to conversational data for you, which turns the role-tagged turns into the exact token layout that model family expects.

7
00:00:47.931 --> 00:00:56.421
Training on the whole sequence spends some of the learning on reproducing your system prompt and your documents, which is rarely the behavior you want.

8
00:00:56.701 --> 00:00:59.240
Week 3 sets these options deliberately.

9
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.920
For now, the dataset decision is to keep the prompt and the answer cleanly separable, which both formats do.

10
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.163
The other default to know is length.

11
00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:17.659
TRL truncates sequences longer than its maximum length (1,024 tokens by default in the version checked) and keeps the start.

12
00:01:17.939 --> 00:01:20.328
For a chat example, the end is the answer.

13
00:01:20.608 --> 00:01:27.285
A long document can therefore become an example whose answer was cut off, and training proceeds without a complaint.

14
00:01:27.565 --> 00:01:31.107
That is why the validator below flags long examples itself.
